+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard boards must show committed status changes in up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, absorb opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, and let a browser recover after being disconnected for up to 20 minutes. Commands already arrive over HTTPS and commit as single authoritative event revisions; the open question was how committed revisions travel from server to browsers. Three mechanisms can meet the freshness target — interval HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service — and the choice sizes three design nodes at once: the change store's publication surface, the delivery component's session model, and the browsers' recovery behavior. No overriding criterion was fixed in advance between operating burden and running cost, so the tradeoff is weighed explicitly below.
+
+Foundational invariants every option respects, and therefore background rather than decision elements: one authoritative revision per committed edit regardless of delivery route; connectivity failures visible rather than mistaken for an unchanged board; server-side authorization independent of delivery.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+No gates eliminate any option: all three meet the 15-second freshness target and the 800-browser concurrency requirement, and the project's infrastructure tenet explicitly permits both self-hosted and managed infrastructure. The choice is decided on weighted criteria:
+
+- **Update latency** (major): how quickly a committed change appears in a connected browser.
+- **Operating burden** (major): connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect work the team runs day to day, including burst fan-out to ~800 concurrent sessions.
+- **Recurring cost** (major): continuing fees or usage charges beyond self-hosted compute.
+- **Vendor independence** (major): whether the update path can be re-implemented without depending on a single provider's service, SDK, and pricing.
+- **Read amplification** (moderate): store reads the mechanism generates at steady state — 800 browsers, roughly two edits per minute, bursts of 20 per second.
+- **Browser-side complexity** (moderate): how much reconnect and catch-up logic the browser must implement.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers re-fetch board changes on a fixed interval; the store answers cursor queries ("everything since revision N"). Its strongest case: simplest stateless operation — every request independent, no long-lived connections, no session lifecycle, trivial horizontal scaling, and standard HTTP infrastructure (load balancers, caches, proxies) works unmodified. Delivery stays request/response; browsers run a plain polling loop.
+
+- Update latency: **weak** — a committed change waits out the remainder of the interval; seconds of lag.
+- Operating burden: **strong** — no connections to manage; stateless requests.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no vendor bill.
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — plain HTTPS.
+- Read amplification: **weak** — every browser re-reads on every interval regardless of change; 800 browsers polling every five seconds is ~9,600 store reads per minute against ~2 changes.
+- Browser-side complexity: **strong** — a loop and a cursor.
+
+Summary: trades freshness and store read load for the simplest possible operations.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds WebSocket sessions open and pushes each committed revision to connected browsers. The store publishes replayable revisions; delivery fans them out and manages sessions; browsers reconnect and catch up by replaying from the last revision they received.
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — push; subsecond under normal connectivity.
+- Operating burden: **weak** — the team owns connection capacity (≈800 concurrent sessions), burst fan-out (20 edits/s × 800 sessions ≈ 16,000 messages per second at peak), session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — self-hosted compute only.
+- Vendor independence: **strong** — no external provider.
+- Read amplification: **strong** — the store is read per change, not per interval.
+- Browser-side complexity: **weak** — reconnect and catch-up logic beyond a plain polling loop.
+
+Summary: trades connection-operations work for fast updates at no vendor cost.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime provider carries the updates: the server publishes to the provider, browsers subscribe through its SDK. The store needs publication reconciliation; delivery wraps the provider.
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — provider push.
+- Operating burden: **partial** — the provider handles connection capacity; the team manages the account, credentials, and reconciling publication with the store.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — recurring fees, typically scaling with connections or messages.
+- Vendor independence: **weak** — the update path is coupled to one provider's service, SDK, and subscription model.
+- Read amplification: **strong** — per-change publication.
+- Browser-side complexity: **partial** — reconnect is the provider's SDK, but its subscription model must be learned and reconciled with board recovery after long disconnects.
+
+Summary: trades money and provider coupling for the least connection-operations work.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It matches the hosted option's strong latency while keeping polling's strong cost and independence profile; what it gives up is the strong operating-burden position. The team accepted owning connection capacity and reconnect handling to get fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
+
+The decision turns on the infrastructure tenet that both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted: vendor coupling was weighed as a cost, not forbidden, and found not worth the operations it would offload at the pilot's single-region scale. Committing to it obligates the downstream nodes: the change store must publish replayable revisions with reliable publication; delivery must manage sessions and fan-out sized for ~800 concurrent connections and 16,000 messages per second at burst; browser sync must make disconnects visible (per the connectivity-failure tenet) and catch up by replaying from the last received revision. It forecloses stateless request/response delivery (polling's simplicity) and vendor-managed connection operations (the hosted service).
